Final Review of the Ambulance Booking System Project
The Ambulance Booking System project is an essential web-based application designed
to streamline the process of booking ambulances for emergency or non-emergency medical
transportation. The system provides users with a platform to book ambulances, view their 
bookings, manage their profiles, and receive confirmation details. 
It integrates various technologies such as Java Servlets, JSP, JSTL, EL, JUnit, and
Mockito to handle different aspects of the application, from the user interface to 
business logic and data persistence.

In this review, we will focus on the following components of the project:

1-System Overview
2-Architecture and Design
3-Key Features and Functionality
4-Technologies Used
5-Implementation
6-Testing and Quality Assurance
7-Scalability and Performance
8-Security Considerations
9-Final Thoughts


1. System Overview
The Ambulance Booking System allows users to book ambulances, view and manage
their bookings, and manage their profiles. It serves as a user-friendly application 
where users can specify their preferences such as ambulance type, time, and 
location. The system enables administrators or support staff to handle requests,
ensuring that users receive timely medical assistance in emergencies or non-emergencies.
The application was developed using a combination of Java-based technologies to handle both
the front-end and back-end aspects of the system.


2. Architecture and Design
The system follows a Model-View-Controller (MVC) architecture, which is widely recognized for separating concerns and enhancing maintainability.

Model: The Model represents the data and business logic of the application. 
In this project, the model consists of JavaBeans such as Booking and User,
which encapsulate the data related to bookings and user profiles. The model
also includes service classes that implement the business logic (e.g., BookingService).
View: The View in this project is implemented using JSP (JavaServer Pages), 
which renders HTML dynamically based on user interactions and data fetched from the back-end. 
JSTL (JavaServer Pages Standard Tag Library) and EL (Expression Language) are used to manage 
dynamic content and logic within the views efficiently.
Controller: The Controller handles user input and orchestrates
interactions between the Model and View. Java Servlets serve as controllers,
processing requests, invoking appropriate service methods, and forwarding results 
to the corresponding JSP pages.

3. Key Features and Functionality
The Ambulance Booking System offers a wide range of features that enhance user 
experience and make the process of booking ambulances smooth and efficient.
User Registration and Login: Users can create an account and log in to the system using their credentials.
Authentication 
ensures that only authorized users can access their profiles and make bookings.

Ambulance Booking: The core feature of the system is allowing users to book an ambulance based on their needs.
They can select the type of ambulance (Basic or Advanced), specify the pickup time, and provide their address
and contact details.
Booking Management: Users can view their bookings, including booking details like the ambulance type, time,
and pickup location. They can also cancel their bookings if needed.

User Profile Management: Users can update their personal details, including name, email, phone number, and address.

Booking Confirmation: Once a booking is successfully made,
the system provides a confirmation page, showing the details of the booked ambulance and the scheduled time.

Admin Functionality: The system may include administrative features to manage users, handle booking requests, and monitor the ambulance fleet. 
However, these features were not fully implemented in this review version.

4. Technologies Used
The following technologies were employed in this project:

Java Servlets: Used to handle HTTP requests, process user input, and communicate with the
database through DAO (Data Access Object) classes.
JSP (JavaServer Pages): Used for rendering dynamic HTML pages with data from the back-end.

JSTL (JavaServer Pages Standard Tag Library): Allows for simplified access to business logic and reduces 
the complexity of JSP pages by replacing scriptlets with tags for iteration, conditional rendering, etc.
EL (Expression Language): A feature of JSP that simplifies the access and manipulation of data.

JUnit: A framework for unit testing Java code. It was used to write test cases for the service and DAO layers.

Mockito: A mocking framework used to mock dependencies, such as the DAO layer, to test the service layer independently.
H2 Database: An in-memory database used for unit testing the DAO layer, simulating interactions with a real database.

HTML, CSS, and JavaScript: Used for designing the user interface and enhancing user interactions.

5. Implementation
The implementation of the Ambulance Booking System involves several key aspects:

Database Interaction: The DAO (Data Access Object) pattern is used to abstract database operations.
The BookingDAO class interacts with the database to save and retrieve booking records. Data such as 
user profiles and ambulance bookings are stored in the database, and CRUD operations are performed by the DAO layer.

Service Layer: The BookingService class contains the business logic for booking an ambulance. It validates user input, 
processes bookings, and communicates with the DAO layer to persist the data.

Servlets and JSP: Servlets act as the controllers, processing requests and forwarding them to appropriate JSP pages. The JSP pages dynamically render user information,
such as booking details, using data passed from servlets.

User Interface: The UI is designed with simplicity and usability in mind, allowing users to easily book ambulances and manage their profiles. Forms are used to collect
user input, and feedback is displayed on confirmation pages.


6. Testing and Quality Assurance
Testing is a crucial part of ensuring the quality of the system. Several unit tests were created for the service and DAO layers using JUnit and Mockito.

Unit Testing: The BookingServiceTest class tests business logic by mocking dependencies like the BookingDAO. The unit tests verify that 
the service logic functions as expected.
DAO Testing: The BookingDAOTest class tests database interactions using an in-memory H2 database. This approach ensures 
that the data access logic works correctly without the need for an actual database server.

Integration Testing: Although not explicitly covered in this review, 
integration tests should be performed to ensure that the system works as a whole, including all components working together 
(UI, backend, database, etc.).

7. Scalability and Performance
The current version of the system is designed to work on a small scale with minimal performance concerns. However, there are
a few areas where scalability can be enhanced:
Database Scaling: If the application needs to handle a larger number of bookings, optimizing database queries, 
using connection pooling, and introducing caching mechanisms would be crucial.
Service Layer Performance: The service layer might need to be refactored to handle large volumes of requests more efficiently, 
especially when validating and processing user data.


